  "helpSearchHint": "Search help topics",
  "helpEmptySearchResult": "No results found",
  "helpStartSectionTitle": "Getting Started",
  "helpStartOnboardingTitle": "The screens you see on first launch",
  "helpStartOnboardingBody": "The first time you open the app, it asks for a few permissions: notifications, exact alarms, and display over other apps. Alarms need them to ring on time, even on the lock screen. If you skip one, alarms may ring late or not at all.\n\nOnce they're granted, you'll move on to creating your work schedule.",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesTitle": "Repeating vs. irregular schedules",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesBody": "You can build your work schedule in two ways.\n\nA repeating schedule follows a fixed pattern, like \"day, day, night, night, off, off, rest\", that repeats from a chosen start date. Once you set the pattern and start date, the app fills in every future date for you.\n\nAn irregular schedule has no repeating pattern, so you assign a shift to each date yourself. Long-press a date on the calendar to assign a shift to that day or to several days at once.",
  "helpShiftSectionTitle": "Managing Shifts",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignTitle": "Assigning shifts as an irregular worker",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignBody": "If you're on an irregular schedule, long-press a date in the Calendar tab and choose which shift you're working that day (or several days at once). It applies only to the dates you pick, not as a repeating pattern, so each date is set individually.\n\nIf you assign shifts a few days ahead, alarms for those days are prepared automatically to match.",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftTitle": "Changing the shift on a specific date",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftBody": "Whether you're on a repeating or irregular schedule, you can override a single date by long-pressing it on the calendar and picking a different shift. Alarms for that day are automatically recalculated to match the new shift.",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsTitle": "Customizing shift colors",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsBody": "Every calendar theme has default colors per shift, but you can override the color of a specific shift name if you like. Pick the shift name in the shift settings and choose a color — your choice takes priority over the theme's default.",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsTitle": "What is the \"all shifts\" view?",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsBody": "This view shows the shift pattern of coworkers on other teams at the same workplace — handy for checking things like \"when is Team A off this week?\"\n\nYou can open it from the Calendar tab. For it to show correctly, you need to set up your team structure first (how many teams there are, and how many days ahead or behind each one rotates relative to you).",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeTitle": "When your team changes",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeBody": "Go to Settings → Edit → Change Schedule and assign today's shift. Your shift names and alarms stay exactly as they are.",
  "helpAlarmSectionTitle": "Alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesTitle": "Creating fixed alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesBody": "You can register up to 5 alarms per shift. They can fire on the shift day itself, the day before, or the day after, and each alarm has its own time, sound, and other settings.",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeTitle": "Changing alarm sounds / volume issues",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeBody": "You can choose from 7 built-in alarm sounds or your device's own system alarm sounds. Speaker characteristics vary by device, so the app auto-corrects volume, but if it still feels off, try switching to a different sound in the alarm settings.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingTitle": "If an alarm doesn't go off",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingBody": "The most common cause is permissions. Check that \"exact alarms\", \"notifications\", and \"display over other apps\" are all enabled for this app, and that it's excluded from battery optimization.\n\nThe next most common cause is the shift or alarm setup. Make sure a shift is actually assigned to that date and that the shift has a fixed alarm registered. If you assign a shift but never create a fixed alarm, nothing will ring.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryTitle": "Viewing alarm history",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryBody": "You can see every alarm and its result under Settings → Alarm History.",
  "helpCalendarSectionTitle": "Calendar & Home Widget",
  "helpCalendarThemesTitle": "Changing the calendar theme",
  "helpCalendarThemesBody": "You can choose from 10 calendar themes. The theme applies to the calendar inside the app. The home-screen widget always uses its default light look.",
  "helpCalendarOtTitle": "Recording and checking overtime",
  "helpCalendarOtBody": "From a date's detail view in the Calendar tab you can log overtime for that day. What you log also feeds into the weekly and monthly work-hours totals.",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayTitle": "Checking weekly and monthly work hours",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayBody": "Enter each shift's clock-in and clock-out times in settings and the app calculates your monthly and weekly work hours automatically. Overtime is included. You can also choose in settings whether the default hours of a shift you change on the calendar count toward OT.",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetTitle": "Adding the home-screen widget",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetBody": "Long-press your home screen, open the widget picker, and add this app's calendar widget. It shows shift names and memos.",
  "helpScheduleTabSectionTitle": "Schedule Tab",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItTitle": "How is the Schedule tab different?",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItBody": "While a calendar memo is a note for the whole day, the Schedule tab lets you manage what you're doing from one specific time to another within a day. It's for things with an actual time attached, like an appointment before work or plans after your shift.",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditTitle": "Creating and editing a schedule item",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditBody": "Tap the clock icon at the bottom right to switch to time-picking mode. Tap again at the time you want and the new schedule screen appears.",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryTitle": "How matching icons get picked",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryBody": "The app reads what you typed and automatically attaches a matching icon. If it can't find a good match, a default shape icon is attached instead.",
  "helpConditionSectionTitle": "Sleep & Recovery",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItTitle": "What is the Sleep & Recovery tab?",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItBody": "This screen links your work schedule with your actual sleep records and, based on the current time, shows confirmed facts (recovery time between shifts, sleep after your shift, consecutive night or long shifts) and recommended actions (when to be in bed, when to stop caffeine, commuting cautions). It does not grade you with a score; if sleep records or shift times are missing, it tells you the limits of what it could judge. Health-related thresholds and advice are grounded in sleep-medicine and occupational-health research.",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListTitle": "Sleep & Recovery evidence",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListBody": "The Sleep & Recovery tab's guidance draws on the 14 sources below. A few thresholds, such as consecutive workdays or comparisons with your own average, are set by this app and don't carry a separate source.\n\nEVIDENCE-001 · Shift rotation direction — Forward rotation (day→evening→night) is reported to be easier to adapt to than backward rotation. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-002 · Minimum rest between shifts — Recommends at least 11 hours of rest between two shifts. (Knauth & Hornberger, cited in NIOSH Module 5)\n\nEVIDENCE-003 · Minimum rest between shifts (legal standard) — Every worker must get at least 11 consecutive hours of rest per 24 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 3)\n\nEVIDENCE-004 · Long shifts and injury/accident risk — Relative risk is modeled at about +13% for a 10-hour shift and +27% for a 12-hour shift, versus an 8-hour shift. (Folkard & Lombardi, 2006)\n\nEVIDENCE-005 · Rest days after consecutive shifts — Recommends 1–2 rest days after 5 days of 8-hour shifts or 4 days of 10-hour shifts, and 2 rest days after 3 days of 12-hour shifts. It also advises against packing work into a few days and then taking several days off. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-006 · Sleep after a night shift — Recommends prioritizing sleep after a night shift. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-007 · Napping before a night shift — Conditionally helpful, but the guideline itself states the evidence level is very low. Watch for grogginess right after waking. (AASM, 2025)\n\nEVIDENCE-008 · Caffeine and sleep — Caffeine taken even 6 hours before bedtime significantly cuts total sleep time by over an hour. (Drake et al., 2013)\n\nEVIDENCE-009 · Light exposure on the commute home after a night shift — Reducing bright light exposure can help later sleep (but safety comes first if you're too drowsy to drive safely). (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-010 · Eating during a night shift — Recommends eating less between midnight and 6am and favoring light meals. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-011 · Recommended sleep for adults — Adults aged 18–64 are recommended 7–9 hours of sleep. (National Sleep Foundation, 2015)\n\nEVIDENCE-012 · Maximum weekly working hours (legal standard) — Average weekly working time, including overtime, must not exceed 48 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 6)\n\nEVIDENCE-013 · Consecutive night shifts and accident risk — Relative risk of accidents/injuries accumulates with each successive night shift (about +6% on night 2, +17% on night 3, +36% on night 4 — the increase is steeper for consecutive night shifts than for consecutive day/evening shifts). (Folkard & Tucker, 2003)\n\nEVIDENCE-014 · Weekly cumulative working hours — Recommends limiting nurses' work to 12 hours in 24 and 60 hours in 7 days. A 2014 systematic review reported that working more than 40 hours a week is linked to lower patient safety. (IOM, 2004; cited in NIOSH Module 5)",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededTitle": "Setup required before using this tab",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededBody": "You need to enter clock-in/out times for at least one shift name under Settings → Work Hours & OT first. Until you do, Today's Condition won't appear, but you can still log sleep. You don't need to fill in every shift — just one you're currently using is enough.",
  "helpSleepSectionTitle": "Sleep Tracking",
  "helpSleepWidgetTitle": "What is the sleep-tracking widget?",
  "helpSleepWidgetBody": "Add the sleep widget to your home screen and tap \"Sleep\" before bed and \"Wake\" after you get up to log your sleep in a couple of taps. The widget also shows today's shift.\n\nThe sleep you log is used in the Sleep & Recovery tab, alongside your work schedule, to show what to look after right now.",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectTitle": "Automatic sleep estimation",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectBody": "Even without touching the widget, the app can guess when you were probably asleep based on your work schedule and how long your phone went untouched. It looks for sleep after work, night sleep, and naps you take before a night shift. Sleep during a shift is not picked up automatically, so log it with the widget.\n\nA detected record is always just an estimate. It becomes an official record once you confirm it.",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarTitle": "Reviewing and editing recent sleep records",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarBody": "The \"Recent Sleep\" mini calendar on the Sleep & Recovery tab shows confirmed records day by day. Tap a slot to create, edit, or delete a record right there. For older records, use \"View All\" to browse month by month.\n\nBelow it, \"My sleep by shift\" breaks down your average sleep for each shift type into main sleep and naps.",
  "helpFriendSectionTitle": "Friend Sharing",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItTitle": "What is friend sharing?",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItBody": "This lets you share your work schedule so a friend or someone else can view it — or view a schedule someone else shared with you. You can share your schedule easily even with people who don't have the app.",
  "helpFriendShareCodeTitle": "Generating and sharing your code",
  "helpFriendShareCodeBody": "Open \"My Share Code\" from settings to generate a code. Give it to the other person, and they can register it in their own app to view your schedule.",
  "helpFriendViewFriendTitle": "Viewing a friend's schedule with their code",
  "helpFriendViewFriendBody": "If you've received a friend's code, add them from the friend list screen by entering it — their schedule then shows up as a calendar inside your own app.",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteTitle": "What happens if you reinstall the app",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteBody": "Reinstalling the app creates what's internally treated as a new account, which breaks any friend-sharing connections you had before. To reconnect, generate a new share code and send it to the other person again.\n\nYour friend won't see any error on their end — their screen will just keep showing your schedule exactly as it was right before you reinstalled. Since they have no way to notice this on their own, be sure to let them know if you reinstall.",
  "helpBackupSectionTitle": "Backup & Restore",
  "helpBackupDataStorageTitle": "Where is my data stored?",
  "helpBackupDataStorageBody": "Things like alarms, your work schedule, memos, schedule items, and sleep records are stored only on your own device. None of it is sent to an external server, and it's gone if you delete the app, which is why backups matter.\n\nThe one exception is friend sharing. When you turn it on, only what's needed to share (shift pattern, colors) is uploaded to a server (Firestore). Memos, overtime, work hours, and alarm settings are never uploaded, even then.",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupTitle": "When does automatic backup happen?",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupBody": "When you leave the app (press home or switch apps), it backs up automatically, but only if something actually changed. The file is saved to the Download/ShiftBell folder on your device and is not sent to the cloud or another device. You can also back up manually with \"Data backup\" in settings.",
  "helpBackupRestoreTitle": "Restoring after reinstalling or moving to a new device",
  "helpBackupRestoreBody": "If you uninstalled and reinstalled the app, tap \"Load\" under \"Used this app before?\" on the first screen and pick the backup file yourself. It's in the Download/ShiftBell folder.\n\nTo move to a new device, first copy the backup file from the old device's Download/ShiftBell folder onto the new one.\n\nAfter restoring, the app asks again for permissions such as notifications and exact alarms, as a safety measure.",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualTitle": "If automatic restore doesn't find your backup",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualBody": "When you uninstall an app, Android doesn't automatically show the files it created to the newly installed copy, so after a reinstall the app can't find your old backup on its own. Use \"Load\" next to \"Used this app before?\" on the first screen to open a file picker, then choose the most recent backup file in the Download/ShiftBell folder yourself.",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailTitle": "A very old backup may fail to restore",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailBody": "The data format the app saves occasionally changes as it is updated. If there is a big version gap between the app that made a backup and the one restoring it (for example, restoring a very old backup into a much newer version), the restore can fail, and retrying with the same file will likely fail for the same reason.\n\nRight before you switch phones or reinstall, tap \"Data backup\" in settings to make a fresh backup. Even if automatic backups have been working fine, doing one manually right before an important moment is safer.",
  "helpTroubleshootSectionTitle": "Troubleshooting",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueTitle": "My alarm didn't go off",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueBody": "First check that notifications, exact alarms, and display-over-other-apps are enabled for this app, and that it's excluded from battery optimization. Then confirm a shift is actually assigned to that date and that the shift has a fixed alarm registered.",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopTitle": "I force-stopped this app from phone settings",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopBody": "\"Force stop\" from Settings → Apps cancels every alarm this app had scheduled. That is different from just leaving the app or swiping it away from recent apps. Neither of those affects alarms.\n\nWhen you reopen the app after a force stop, any alarm whose time hasn't passed yet is registered again automatically. But an alarm whose time passed between the force stop and the moment you reopen the app is not restored and won't ring that day.\n\nSo if you force-stopped the app, open it once before your next alarm is due. It will then ring normally again.",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueTitle": "The widget isn't updating",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueBody": "Try removing the widget and adding it again. If it still looks the same, open the app once, then go back to your home screen and check again.",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingTitle": "I can't find my backup file",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingBody": "Check the Download/ShiftBell folder directly using your device's file manager app. If the folder doesn't exist, no backup has been made yet, so tap \"Data backup\" in settings to create one.",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedTitle": "My friend's schedule hasn't changed in days",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedBody": "If the schedule looks frozen with no error shown, it's almost always because your friend reinstalled the app. Reinstalling creates a new internal account, which breaks the existing connection. Nothing on screen tells you that happened, and it keeps showing whatever it last had.\n\nCheck the \"Last synced\" time at the top of your friend's schedule screen. If it's stuck on an old date, that's the sign. Ask your friend for a new share code, then remove the old entry and add them again with it.",

